  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1038 PM CDT MON JUL 27 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening reveals a strengthening upper\nlevel ridge across the Four Corners region and an upper trough\npushing through the Great Lakes region. Several shortwaves will\nrotate around the periphery of the high, which will keep low to\nmedium chances for showers and storms in the forecast. Tuesday's\nactivity looks to remain east of the region as the ridge shifts\nslightly east across the Southern Plains. By mid-week, the ridge\nbegins to retreat south and west as the trough along the East\nCoast deepens and pushes a front through the region. This will \nallow the aforementioned shortwaves to inch closer to the region, \nbringing better chances for showers and storms, generally to the\nsouth of the front, through Thursday. Another upper trough cuts \nacross the Plains into the Ohio Valley over the weekend. This will\nsend a front our way, leading to increased rain chances over the \nweekend.\n\nThe main story over the next couple of days will be our\nincreasing heat risk. Temps in the upper 90s along with muggy\nconditions will warrant a major heat risk across most of the\nregion Tuesday with heat indices approaching 110 degrees. The\nlatest ECMWF EFI hints at an unusually warm day tomorrow,\nespecially across the southern half of the CWA. Highs tomorrow\nwill be about 10 degrees warmer than usual for this time of the\nyear. As a result, the Heat Advisory remains in effect through\nTuesday evening. We will knock off a couple of degrees by mid\nweek as the ridge retreats back west. High temps look to settle\ninto the upper 80s to low 90s by Friday.\n\n95/Castillo",
